    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sliding along a pre-existing flaw or discontinuity in a rock mass can lead to local stress concentrations along the flaw that may exceed the rock strength and produce secondary structural features. This process occurs over a range of scales from centimeters to hundreds of kilometers. At the outcrop scale, secondary features may be joints and solution surfaces; at the crustal scale stress concentrations may produce normal faults and pressure ridges, folds and thrust faults. The geometry of these secondary features may have important implications for fluid flow around the discontinuity and the interpretation of seismic and subseismic faults.\r\n\r\nAs a large scale example of strike-slip related deformation, we consider the distribution and\r\norientation of lineaments in the southwest region of Lavinia Planitia, Venus, which are similar to the\r\ngeometries of normal faults formed as secondary structures of strike-slip faulting. We propose that the observed lineaments are fractures formed as the result of shearing localized along a deformation belt which acted as a pre-existing flaw.\r\n\r\nIn this paper we detail the structural observations from Lavinia Planitia and compare\r\nthem to terrestrial examples of strike-slip faulting. The orientations, lengths, and distribution of observed secondary features depend on factors such as initial flaw geometry, remote stress orientation and magnitude, friction along the discontinuity, and slip gradient along the discontinuity. We use boundary element techniques to model the deformation belt as a fault and investigate the effects of varying remote stresses and friction on the stress\r\nfield around the fault.",
